# Line endings.
#
# The repository is checked out on Windows with core.autocrlf=true, which rewrites
# LF to CRLF in the working tree. That is harmless for most files but fatal for the
# two below:
#
#   entrypoint.sh  is COPYed into a Linux image and executed via its shebang. A CRLF
#                  file makes the kernel look for "/bin/bash\r", and the container
#                  dies at startup with "bad interpreter: No such file or directory".
#                  The same applies to any script under scripts/.
#   Dockerfile     is parsed by BuildKit; keep it LF so line continuations and the
#                  parser's expectations are never in question.
#
# `eol=lf` pins the working-tree copy to LF regardless of core.autocrlf, so a local
# `docker compose up --build` on Windows produces the same image as CI does.
*.sh          text eol=lf
Dockerfile    text eol=lf
*.dockerfile  text eol=lf

# This file too: a stray CR on the last line would become part of the pattern.
.gitattributes text eol=lf

# Config and docs: normalise to LF in the repository, let git decide the checkout.
*.yml         text
*.yaml        text
*.json        text
*.js          text
*.md          text
